Because \(\eta>0\) and \(\rho\ge0\), the mixing probability \(q=\rho/(\eta+\rho)\) lies in \([0,1)\).  For every \(v\in V\) and \(h\in D_v\), reporting \(V\) always covers \(v\).  Therefore
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 P_h\{v\in K^+(W)\}
 &\ge q+(1-q)(1-\eta-\rho)\\
 &=\frac{\rho+\eta(1-\eta-\rho)}{\eta+\rho}
 =1-\eta.
 \end{aligned} \tag{12}
\]
Taking the two infima in (12) proves \(K^+\in\mathcal C_\eta\).  Since \(0\le\ell(A)\le D\) for every action and \(\ell(V)=D\), for every \(h\in H_r\),
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 E_h\ell\{K^+(W)\}
 &=(1-q)E_h\ell\{\widetilde K(W)\}+qD\\
 &\le E_h\ell\{\widetilde K(W)\}+qD.
 \end{aligned} \tag{13}
\]
Taking the supremum in (13) and substituting \(q\) proves the risk inequality.

Fix \(g\in P_{v,b}^0\).  Component feasibility gives \((A_{v,b}^0)_{i\cdot}g\le(b_{v,b}^0)_i\); hence
\[
 \gamma
 \le (A_{v,b}^0)_{i\cdot}(u_*-g). \tag{21}
\]
Apply Cauchy--Schwarz after inserting \(\Sigma_0^{1/2}\Sigma_0^{-1/2}\):
\[
 \begin{aligned}
 (A_{v,b}^0)_{i\cdot}(u_*-g)
 &=\{(A_{v,b}^0)_{i\cdot}\Sigma_0^{1/2}\}
   \{\Sigma_0^{-1/2}(u_*-g)\}\\
 &\le \|(A_{v,b}^0)_{i\cdot}\|_{\Sigma_0}
       \|u_*-g\|_{\Sigma_0^{-1}}
 \le s\|u_*-g\|_{\Sigma_0^{-1}}.
 \end{aligned} \tag{22}
\]
Positive definiteness of \(\Sigma_0\) and \(\gamma>0\) imply that the selected row is nonzero and hence that its \(\Sigma_0\)-norm is positive.  Divide (21)--(22) by \(s>0\) and take the infimum over \(g\in P_{v,b}^0\).

